{
  "reasoning": "The evaluation failed due to instance state mismatch in the GorillaFileSystem across turns. The model executed exploratory commands (pwd, cd, ls) in Turn 0 and chained multiple operations (wc, cat, echo, sort, wc) in Turn 3, violating the atomic operation principle and causing file system state divergence from ground truth.",
  "error_identification": "Turn 0: Model chained pwd→cd→ls→cp→cd→mv→ls instead of executing one atomic operation (cp then mv). Turn 2: Model executed wc→cat→echo→sort→wc in sequence, creating intermediate state mutations and re-sorting an already-sorted file, rather than executing a single wc call to count lines.",
  "root_cause_analysis": "The model violated delta10 (no exploratory command chains), delta14 (no multiple echo calls), and delta9 (one atomic operation per turn). In Turn 0, navigation commands should have been omitted; the cp and mv should execute atomically without intervening ls/cd. In Turn 2, only wc should execute—the cat/echo/sort chain created spurious state changes that don't match ground truth file system state.",
  "correct_approach": "Turn 0: Execute cp atomically (source: research_notes.txt, destination: archives/2024_research_backup.txt) or use mv with full path; skip pwd/cd/ls. Turn 1: Execute sort atomically on the file. Turn 2: Execute only wc with mode 'l' to count lines; yield immediately without cat, echo, or re-sort.",
  "key_insight": "Multi-turn file system tasks require strict adherence to one atomic state-mutating operation per turn. Exploratory commands (ls, pwd, cat) and chained transformations create divergent state; the model must trust tool execution and await the next user request rather than validating or re-processing intermediate results."
}

